from django.utils import timezone

class HikeQuerySet(models.QuerySet):
    def update(self, **kwargs):
        kwargs.setdefault('updated_at', timezone.now())
        return super().update(**kwargs)

class Hike(models.Model):
    ...
    updated_at = models.DateTimeField(auto_now=True)
    objects = HikeQuerySet.as_manager()
```
Right: `auto_now` is applied in `Model.save()` (in `pre_save` of the field), and `QuerySet.update()` goes straight to SQL without calling `save()`, so it never fires. Overriding `update` on a custom QuerySet covers every `.filter(...).update(...)` that goes through `Hike.objects`. `bulk_update()` won't go through it, so pass `updated_at` in its fields yourself. If you want it airtight even for raw SQL, a Postgres trigger is the only thing that catches everything.

